adjudication.rationale:
In `checkIfIsAvailable`, the new working-hours logic uses `workingHours.find(...)` to return `true` (and thus reject the slot) when the slot is outside *any* `workingHour` entry matching the weekday:
```ts
if (workingHours.find((workingHour) => {
  if (workingHour.days.includes(slotStartTime.day())) {
    ...
    if (start < workingHour.startTime || end > workingHour.endTime) {
      return true;
    }
  }
})) return false;
```
With multiple ranges on the same day (split schedules/lunch breaks or ranges split across UTC midnight), a slot that is valid in one range will typically be outside another range, causing `find` to succeed and the slot to be incorrectly rejected. This matches the finding and is a correctness defect introduced by this diff.

In `checkIfIsAvailable`, the working-hours rejection logic computes both `start` and `end` from `slotStartTime`:
```ts
const start = slotStartTime.hour() * 60 + slotStartTime.minute();
const end = slotStartTime.hour() * 60 + slotStartTime.minute();
if (start < workingHour.startTime || end > workingHour.endTime) return true;
```
Because `end` is not based on `slotEndTime` (which is computed earlier as `time.add(eventLength, "minutes").utc()`), slots that start within working hours but extend past `workingHour.endTime` will not be rejected. This matches the finding and is a correctness defect introduced/exposed by this diff.

In `checkIfIsAvailable` (new working-hours logic), the code computes both `start` and `end` from `slotStartTime`:
```ts
const start = slotStartTime.hour() * 60 + slotStartTime.minute();
const end = slotStartTime.hour() * 60 + slotStartTime.minute();
```
This makes `end` identical to `start`, so the check `end > workingHour.endTime` will never catch slots that *start* before the working-hours end but *end* after it (i.e., slots straddling the boundary). The diff also adds no test covering such a straddling-slot case; the only new test relates to date overrides/timezones.
